text:
linh: quick brain dump — thinking aloud about a small, cooperative backpressure adapter between admission control and the serving runtime. idea: runtime can request soft reservations (short ttl) instead of being fully blocked; admission layer can grant, deny, or downgrade. would reduce hard failures during short spikes. any immediate red flags?

maya: i like the idea. key Q: what contract do we want? sync RPC vs async token stream? sync gives simpler correctness, async fits bursty flows.

sam: +1. also we should ensure metrics for accept/deny/downgrade and per-tenant latency impact are exposed. otherwise we can't validate improvements.

linh: proposal sketch: lightweight gRPC Reserve API with TTL and optional priority. runtime asks for N tokens for X ms. admission responds with allowed_tokens, offered_tier (full|soft|best-effort), reason. soft means we still count it but may preempt later.

linh: proto sketch:
```proto
service Backpressure {
  rpc Reserve(ReserveReq) returns (ReserveResp) {}
}
message ReserveReq {
  string tenant_id = 1;
  int32 tokens = 2;
  int32 ttl_ms = 3;
  int32 priority = 4; // higher => more likely to get full grant
}
message ReserveResp {
  int32 granted = 1;
  enum Tier { FULL = 0; SOFT = 1; BEST_EFFORT = 2; }
  Tier tier = 2;
  string note = 3;
}
```

yasser: danger: soft reservations can be gamed if clients shift load to short bursts to bypass quotas. need abuse signals (request pattern entropy, repeated short ttl requests).

rajeev: agreed. we can add enforcement windows: if tenant does repeated soft requests > X/s, we start penalizing priority or reduce burst factor. Also tie to credit accounting: soft grants still decrement a short-living reservoir.

heidi: ops perspective: we need clear visibility in Console — per-tenant "soft grants" vs "hard grants" over time. alert when soft grants > 30% of tenant's effective capacity for > 5m.

maya: tradeoff thought: soft grants reduce user-visible errors, but increase complexity for fallbacks in model selection. runtime must be prepared to downgrade model or reduce beam_size when receiving partial grants. that adds coupling.

linh: yeah — interface should include an optional recommended degrade hint so runtime can make deterministic decisions: e.g., {reduce_temperature: false, max_tokens: 512, model_family: "small"}. keep it simple.

sam: operational metrics I'm adding to the tracking doc: reserve_requests_total, reserve_granted_total, reserve_denied_total, reserve_downgraded_total, avg_grant_latency_ms, soft_grant_ratio. Also histogram for ttl_ms.

ops-bot: deploy: backpressure-adapter v0.1 rolled to staging at 2026-03-18T10:12Z :rocket:

yasser: we should also simulate adversarial clients in chaos tests: rapid reserve/release cycles, alternating tenant_ids, mixed priority values. validate backpressure controller doesn't oscillate.

rajeev: algorithm idea: use a decaying reservoir per-tenant + global headroom tax. grant = min(requested, floor(reservoir * burst_factor)) where reservoir decays slowly; soft grants allowed up to some safety multiplier.

linh: sounds good. one last open question: where to place the adapter? as a sidecar to runtime or centralized admission service? sidecar keeps latency low and easier local caching; central gives global view for fairness. maybe hybrid: sidecars do fast path, central adjudicates slow path and long-term fairness.

maya: hybrid makes sense. sidecar gRPC -> local cache -> forward to central as needed. central pushes policies via config stream.

linh: action items:
- linh: write RFC draft for Reserve API + state machine (this week)
- sam: add metrics and dashboards proto (by EOD)
- yasser: chaos test plan for soft-grant abuse (next sprint)
- heidi: console alert thresholds draft (tomorrow)

maya: thanks. if we can keep the runtime contract minimal and make abuse detection incremental, i'm +1 to pilot in staging.

linh: cool, i'll start the RFC. ping me if you want to pair on the proto.
